    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the insurance law, in relation to authorizing the superintendent of financial services to suspend crediting of underwriting earnings for a fiscal year for the purposes of accumulating a subscriber's operating reserve",
    "basePrintNo": "S4599",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4599-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 60,
       "fullName": "Marc Panepinto",
       "imgName": "894_marc_panepinto.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 894,
       "sessionMemberId": 888,
       "sessionYear": 2015,
       "shortName": "PANEPINTO"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §6109, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4599\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the insurance law, in relation to\nauthorizing the superintendent of financial services to suspend\ncrediting of underwriting earnings for a fiscal year for the purposes\nof accumulating a subscriber's operating reserve\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL: To authorize the Superintendent of\nthe Department of Financial Services to suspend continued funding of\nsubscriber operating reserves in appropriate circumstances.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 would amend section 6109 of the insurance law entitled\n\"Subscriber's operating reserve\". Section 2 of the bill provides for\nan immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW: Under current law, the superintendent may approve\nalternative methods for accumulating Subscriber operating reserves\nupon application from a reciprocal's Attorney-in-Fact. However, the\nexisting law does not specifically provide that the Superintendent's\ndiscretion would include suspension of further crediting of\nunderwriting earnings to a Subscribers operating reserve.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: Municipal Insurance Reciprocals are licensed by the\nState to provide a long term stable and secure insurance market for\nNew York States local governments. A priority of municipal reciprocals\nis to avoid the volatility in pricing which is an anathema for\nmunicipal budgets.\n\nAn existing requirement of the Insurance Law's statutory provisions\nfor financing and funding of all reciprocals, including municipal\nreciprocals since 1986, requires 25% of each year underwriting\nearnings to be credited to member operating reserves. A Subscriber\nmay, however, upon withdrawal from membership, and after giving notice\nto the Attorney-in-Fact, withdraw the amount of its operating reserve.\nAs the amounts in municipal subscriber operating reserves have build\nup over time, they can provide an artificial incentive for New York's\nlocal governments to exit a municipal reciprocal insurer, if only to\naccess those funds which have accumulated in the operating reserve.\nThis likelihood is even more acute in the current fiscal environment\nwhere municipal budgets are subject to a property tax cap and to\nexploding expenditures for pension and health benefits which municipal\nleaders and legislatures have virtually no control over.\n\nThe New York Municipal Insurance Reciprocal (NYMIR) has raised this\nissue with the Department over the past several years and inquired\nwhether the Superintendent could exercise the existing (\"approve other\nmethods\") authority pursuant to section 6109 (a) (3) to address this\nproblem which will only continue to grow as NYMIR matures further. The\nDepartment has concluded that the existing statute provides\ninsufficient authority to permit the Superintendent to suspend further\ncontribution to member operating reserves. This legislation would\nspecifically authorize the Superintendent, on an annual basis and only\nwhen requested by the Attorney-in-Fact for a municipal reciprocal, to\nsuspend further crediting of such underwriting earnings to operating\nreserves, and to instead redirect those funds directly to municipal\n\n\nsubscribers in the year immediately following the fiscal year for\nwhich said underwriting earnings were generated.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: New Bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None, except that it would have a salutatory\neffect on the more than 860 local government members of NYMIR in any\nfuture year in which the reciprocal generates underwriting earnings,\nand the Superintendent would agree that the financial position of\nNYMIR (currently A Excellent rated by AM Best) would support a direct\nreturn of earnings to members.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: Immediately.\n\n",
